捷跑电子科技有限公司诉青岛海信进出口有限公司国际货物买卖合同纠纷案

JAPON ELEKTRONIK TEKNOLOJI TICARET LIMITED SIRKETI v. Qingdao Hisense Import & Export Co., Ltd. — dispute over international sales contract

【裁判摘要】

Ruling Summary

I. The "quality requirements" under the warranty against defects in the subject matter, in an international sales contract, normally include the technical standards and quality requirements stipulated by the exporting country and the importing country. In circumstances where the buyer and seller have not made clear and specific agreement on quality standards and requirements in advance, due to the comparability of the items and technical specifications between the pre-delivery and post-delivery inspections, changes observed between the inspections conducted before and after delivery can more pertinently reflect the quality condition of the products.

II. The product recall system aims to prevent the occurrence and expansion of harm through the recall itself, without requiring actual damage as a prerequisite, and the content of recall measures is diverse. With respect to the risk prevention and control corresponding to a product recall, where the product has already been exported, the party who can more conveniently and effectively reasonably prevent and eliminate the risk shall take corresponding measures promptly and correctly.

Plaintiff (Counterclaim Defendant): JAPON ELEKTRONIK TEKNOLOJI TICARET LIMITED SIRKETI (Jiepao Electronic Technology Co., Ltd.) Domicile: Istanbul Eminonü Tomruk Sok. Boyacı Han, Republic of Turkey.
Legal Representative: YILMAZ TAS, Manager of the company.

Defendant (Counterclaim Plaintiff): Qingdao Hisense Import & Export Co., Ltd. Domicile: Tuanjie Road, Qingdao Economic and Technological Development Zone, Shandong Province, People's Republic of China.
Legal Representative: Lin Lan, Chairman of the company.

The Plaintiff (Counterclaim Defendant), JAPON ELEKTRONIK TEKNOLOJI TICARET LIMITED SIRKETI (hereinafter referred to as Jiepao Company), filed a lawsuit with the Qingdao Intermediate People's Court of Shandong Province against the Defendant (Counterclaim Plaintiff), Qingdao Hisense Import & Export Co., Ltd. (hereinafter referred to as Hisense Company), concerning a dispute over an international sales contract.

Plaintiff Jiepao Company alleged: In January 2008, the Plaintiff communicated by telephone and visited the Defendant Hisense Company twice for face-to-face negotiations, ordering 5,000 telephones from the Defendant at USD 28 per unit. Given the Defendant's scale and reputation, the Plaintiff only orally agreed with the Defendant on the time and method of the advance payment, without signing a written contract. Through HSBC Bank USA, the Plaintiff transferred USD 85,000 to the Defendant on February 11, 2008, and transferred USD 28,000 and USD 32,000 in two installments on July 9, 2008. In total, the Plaintiff transferred USD 145,000 to the Defendant (equivalent to RMB 1,023,865.5 at the then exchange rate). After receiving the payment, the Defendant delivered 900 telephones to the Plaintiff through a third party, but all were non-conforming, and the Turkish Telecommunications Authority demanded the recall of all telephones. To protect the Plaintiff's legitimate rights and interests, the Plaintiff requested the court to order the Defendant to refund the payment of RMB 1,023,865.5 (calculated based on the RMB/USD exchange rate of 7.1955 on February 11, 2008, and 6.8708 on July 9, 2008), pay interest (calculated from February 11, 2008, and July 9, 2008, respectively, at the loan interest rate of the People's Bank of China for the corresponding period, provisionally up to the date of filing the lawsuit) amounting to RMB 74,854.5, and pay the translation fees and notarization fees incurred for this case, totaling RMB 6,555.

To prove its claims, Plaintiff Jiepao Company submitted the following evidence:
1. Relevant interbank remittance SWIFT messages;
2. Market Surveillance Laboratory Test Results List;
3. Test Results Report from the Technical Specifications and Standardization Department of the Information and Communication Technologies Authority of Turkey;
4. Unsafe Condition Report;
5. Market Surveillance Laboratory Test Costs List;
6. Notice from the Technical Specifications and Standardization Department of the Information and Communication Technologies Authority of Turkey regarding recall of equipment, payment of fines, and deposit of test costs;
7. Administrative Fine Decision;
8. Notice of Deposit of Administrative Fine;
9. Expert Report;
10. Translation fee invoices, notarization fee invoices;
11. Relevant translation fee invoices;
12. EC Declaration of Conformity of Qingdao Hisense International Marketing Co., Ltd.;
16. Statement of Opinion for Certification Entity by Bay Area Compliance Laboratories Corp.

Defendant Hisense Company argued: The Defendant has strictly performed its delivery obligations in accordance with the contract. The quantity of telephones ordered by Plaintiff Jiepao Company from the Defendant was 70,000 units, not the 5,000 units claimed by the Plaintiff. The Defendant delivered 900 telephones to the Plaintiff on July 25, 2008, as agreed, but the Plaintiff delayed in notifying the Defendant to continue production and shipment. The 70,000 telephones ordered by the Plaintiff were specifically designed and manufactured by the Defendant for the Plaintiff according to EU standards. The Defendant incurred substantial research and development costs. Due to the Plaintiff's breach of contract by not strictly performing its contractual obligations, the Defendant suffered significant economic losses. The court should dismiss the Plaintiff's claims according to law.

Defendant Hisense Company counterclaimed: In November 2007, the Defendant and Plaintiff Jiepao Company entered into a telephone sales contract, stipulating that the Plaintiff would order 70,000 mobile phones from the Defendant, to be specifically designed and manufactured by the Defendant for the Plaintiff. Upon receiving this order, the Defendant invested substantial human and material resources in research, development, and production. On July 25, 2008, the Defendant delivered the first batch of 900 mobile phones to the Plaintiff and awaited the Plaintiff's notification to produce and deliver the remaining phones. However, after receiving the initial 900 phones, the Plaintiff never notified the Defendant to produce and deliver the remaining phones. The Plaintiff's breach of contract caused the Defendant a loss of prospective profits amounting to RMB 5,221,887. Due to the difficulties associated with judicial assistance and cross-border enforcement, the Defendant filed this counterclaim to the extent enforceable, reserving the right to sue for other losses. The counterclaim requested the court to order the Plaintiff to compensate the Defendant for economic losses of RMB 511,462.35.

To prove its claims, Defendant Hisense Company submitted the following evidence:
1. Packing list;
2. Export goods customs declaration;
3. Air waybill;
4. Relevant commercial invoices;
5. Notarial deed of preservation of evidence regarding the browsing and printing of emails and other page contents stored on the computer;
6. Notarial deed of preservation of evidence regarding the browsing and printing of emails stored on the computer and MSN chat records downloaded to the computer;
7. Statement of Opinion for Certification Entity and qualification certificates of Bay Area Compliance Laboratories Corp.;
8. M7 Digital Mobile Phone Contract.

Plaintiff Jiepao Company orally responded: There was no specific purchase contract. The mobile phones were not specifically manufactured for the Plaintiff by Defendant Hisense Company. Due to raw material issues and missing components, the mobile phones were unusable. Customer complaints were numerous. The mobile phones were found to be non-conforming upon testing by the relevant Turkish authorities and were recalled in Turkey.

Facts Found by the Court of First Instance, Qingdao Intermediate People's Court of Shandong Province:

I. Facts regarding the payment of the purchase price

On February 11, 2008, Plaintiff Jiepao Company remitted USD 85,000 to Defendant Hisense Company's bank account through the relevant bank. On July 9, 2008, the Plaintiff remitted USD 28,000 and USD 32,000 in two installments to the Defendant's bank account through the relevant bank.

II. Facts regarding product preparation and product delivery

On March 3, 2008, Defendant Hisense Company entered into an M7 Digital Mobile Phone Contract with Shenzhen Obes Electronic Co., Ltd. The contract stipulated that Shenzhen Obes Electronic Co., Ltd. would design and develop the contract product according to the Defendant's requirements, manufacture it as a complete unit, perform all production tests, complete individual packaging, and then provide it to the Defendant for sale. The Defendant undertook to purchase no less than 20,000 units of the contract product from Shenzhen Obes Electronic Co., Ltd., and the minimum quantity per batch for mass production orders after the trial production was no less than 4,000 units. The tax-exclusive price of the contract product was USD 71/unit. The Defendant paid RMB 750,000 in product research and development fees to Shenzhen Obes Electronic Co., Ltd., with an initial payment of RMB 400,000 (including tax) due within two days after the contract was signed.

On July 19, 2008, Defendant Hisense Company issued a packing list for 900 units of M7 model "HISENSE GSM MOBILE PHONE". The above products were transported from Huanggang Customs in Shenzhen, China, to Hong Kong, China, on July 26, 2008, and then exported by air to the Plaintiff by Hisense International (HK) Co., Limited through Baolong Freight Co., Ltd. on July 27, 2008. Hisense International (HK) Co., Limited issued a commercial invoice to the Plaintiff numbered CMD1108005A, stating a unit price of USD 28/unit, whereas the Defendant's export customs declaration unit price was USD 82/unit.

III. Facts regarding product inspection prior to shipment

On July 8, 2008, Qingdao Hisense International Marketing Co., Ltd. applied to Bay Area Compliance Laboratories Corp. (BACL) located in the United States to certify the SUPERFONE M7 GSM/GPRS mobile phones (serial numbers B1810, network interface GSM900/DCS1800) (frequency bands: GSM900: 880~915MH (TX), 925~960 MH (RX); DCS1800: 1710~1785MH (TX), 1805~1880 MH (RX); BLUETOOTH: 2402~2480 MHz (TX/ RX)). BACL tested the phones for radio spectrum, electromagnetic compatibility, safety, and human health effects according to the specifications/standards EN 300 328 v1.7.1 (2006-10) and EN 301 511 v9.0.2 (2003-03), EN 301 489-17 v1.2.1 (2002-08) and EN 301 489-7 v1.3.1 (2005-11), EN 60950-1:2006, EN 50360:2001, respectively. The certification opinion stated that, according to Directive 1999/5/EC, the wireless equipment and communication equipment complied with the requirements of the Directive and were deemed to pass. On June 15, 2008, Qingdao Hisense International Marketing Co., Ltd. issued an EC Declaration of Conformity, declaring that when installed in accordance with the installation instructions in the product documentation, the products comply with the provisions of Directive 1999/5/EC on Radio and Telecommunications Terminal Equipment (R&TTE).

IV. Facts regarding recall measures implemented by relevant authorities in the importing country for the imported products

Pursuant to relevant Turkish laws, relevant market surveillance regulations, regulations on the surveillance of radiocommunications and telecommunications terminal equipment, and relevant approval documents, the prepared sample report and three samples of SUPERFONE brand, M7 model, dual SIM and GSM mobile phones imported by the Plaintiff were sent to the Technical Specifications and Standardization Department. The Istanbul Regional Directorate held a market surveillance and control meeting on June 1, 2009, at the premises of the importer, JAPON ELEKTRONIK TEKNOLOJI TICARET LIMITED SIRKETI. Samples of SUPERFONE brand, M7 model, with electronic identification codes (IMEI) 355166020008751-355166020008769, 355166020006334-355166020006342, and 355166020004271-355166020004289 underwent several tests at the Market Surveillance Laboratory for Electromagnetic Compatibility (EMC), Radio Frequency (RF), Low Voltage Directive (LVD), and Specific Absorption Rate (SAR). The test results issued by the Technical Specifications and Standardization Department of the Information and Communication Technologies Authority of Turkey were as follows: Devices with IMEI codes 355166020008751-355166020008769 and 355166020006334-355166020006342 failed to pass the provisions of EN 301 489-01 v1.6.1:2005-09, EN 301 489-07 v1.3.1:2005-11 and EN 55022:1998+A1:2000+A2:2003 for emission standards; the output power standard of EN 301 511 v9.0.2:2003-03; and the provisions of "1.7 Marking and Indication" (excluding 1.7.11/13) of the TS EN 60950-1 standard. Devices with IMEI codes 355166020004271-355166020004289 failed to pass the output power standard of EN 301 511 v9.0.2:2003-03 and the provisions of "1.7 Marking and Indication" (excluding 1.7.11/13) of the TS EN 60950-1 standard. Given the non-conformities detected in the test results of the sample experiments, a Non-Safety Test Report was drafted pursuant to the market surveillance and control regulations for radiocommunications and telecommunications terminal equipment, based on the experimental results of the test samples. The test results list prepared by the Market Surveillance Laboratory on August 19, 2009, detailed the test name, test room, test date, test standard, and test result for each test item. The testing costs amounted to 62,524.83 Turkish Lira. On August 20, 2009, the Technical Specifications and Standardization Department of the Information and Communication Technologies Authority of Turkey issued an Unsafe Condition Report regarding the equipment imported by the importer JAPON ELEKTRONIK TEKNOLOJI TICARET LIMITED SIRKETI, namely GSM type, SUPERFONE brand, M7 model, serial numbers/IMEIs as above, which were sampled and tested. The Unsafe Condition Report was prepared based on the relevant content of the test results report, with remarks on the test results and the test results list attached.

On August 26, 2009, the Technical Specifications and Standardization Department of the Information and Communication Technologies Authority of Turkey issued an Administrative Fine Decision. Based on the Unsafe Condition Report, and because the equipment imported by Plaintiff Jiepao Company failed to comply with the provisions of Law No. 4703 on Non-conformity and the relevant articles of the Market Surveillance and Control Regulations for Radiocommunications and Telecommunications Terminal Equipment published in the Official Gazette, a fine of 25,000 Turkish Lira was imposed. The Plaintiff was notified to deposit this administrative fine.

On August 28, 2009, the Technical Specifications and Standardization Department of the Information and Communication Technologies Authority of Turkey notified Plaintiff Jiepao Company in writing that, due to the prohibition on supplying the equipment resulting in the interruption of the equipment's entry into the market, all equipment already supplied to the market must be recalled. The Plaintiff was required to submit a CD-ROM containing the IMEI codes/serial numbers of all recalled equipment to the Information and Communication Technologies Authority, submit the addresses of all stores where the recalled equipment was sold, execute the administrative fine decision, and deposit the testing costs.

Previously, on February 3, 2009, Plaintiff Jiepao Company had commissioned the Turkish Chamber of Commerce to test 455 units out of the 900 M7 model, SUPERFONE brand mobile phones (GSM) imported on July 29, 2008, from Hisense International (HK) Co., Limited, using a sampling method, and an Expert Report was issued. The Expert Report stated that due to manufacturing defects, the Plaintiff voluntarily announced a product recall, and all products were not to be used before testing.

Plaintiff Jiepao Company stated that the products had been recalled and were stored in a warehouse in Turkey.

The issues in dispute in this case are: Whether the products delivered by Defendant Hisense Company to Plaintiff Jiepao Company through a third party had quality issues, and how the civil liability should be allocated.

The Court of First Instance, Qingdao Intermediate People's Court of Shandong Province, held:

Plaintiff Jiepao Company is incorporated in Turkey, and the subject matter of the sale is located outside China. This case is a dispute over an international sales contract. The jurisdictional dispute in this case has already been decided by the High People's Court of Shandong Province of the People's Republic of China in Civil Ruling (2010) Lu Min Xia Zhong Zi No. 32, which confirmed that this Court has jurisdiction over this case. Article 145(1) of the *General Principles of the Civil Law of the People's Republic of China* stipulates: "Parties to a contract involving foreign interests may choose the law applicable to the settlement of contract disputes, except as otherwise provided by law." Both the Plaintiff and the Defendant chose to apply the law of the People's Republic of China during the proceedings, which complies with the above legal provision. The Qingdao Intermediate People's Court of Shandong Province determined the law of the People's Republic of China as the *lex causae* for resolving the dispute in this case.

The Plaintiff and the Defendant have no dispute regarding the establishment of an international sales contract relationship between them, and there is no disagreement on the names of the contracting parties, the subject matter, and the quantity (referring to the actually performed part). Therefore, it can be determined that the legal relationship of the sales contract was established. Whether the Plaintiff and the Defendant had ever concluded a contract in writing is disputed between the parties, but the existence of this disagreement does not affect the final resolution of the case and may be set aside. The legal relationship of the sales contract between the Plaintiff and the Defendant does not have any invalidating circumstances under the laws and administrative regulations of China, is protected by law, and is legally binding on the parties.

Article 148 of the Contract Law of the PRC provides: "If the quality of the subject matter does not meet the quality requirements, thereby preventing the realization of the purpose of the contract, the buyer may reject the subject matter or rescind the contract. If the buyer rejects the subject matter or rescinds the contract, the risk of damage to or loss of the subject matter shall be borne by the seller." The "quality requirements" in the warranty liability for defects in the subject matter under this article, in the context of an international contract for the sale of goods, include not only the technical standards and quality requirements prescribed by the exporting country but also those prescribed by the importing country. On this basis and premise, the parties to the contract may agree on internationally prevailing product standards and technical requirements or make other special stipulations. In this case, the plaintiff, Jiepao Company, claimed that in early 2008, it identified with the "Hisense" exhibit at a trade fair, communicated by telephone, and after two visits to the defendant, Hisense Company, for face-to-face discussions, decided to order the products. The defendant, however, claimed that it had invested substantial manpower and resources specifically to design and manufacture products for the plaintiff. Regardless of whether the procurement is based on an exhibit or an order placed for research, development, and production, it is necessary to reach a consensus on the sale of the goods before performance, especially for cross-national and cross-regional international sales of goods. Where different countries and regions may adopt different quality standards and requirements for products, particularly for special industries including mobile phones, it should be necessary and correct to make clear and specific agreements on the quality standards and requirements of the products in advance. However, based on the evidence submitted by both parties in this case, neither party submitted evidence of a clear and specific agreement made in advance on quality standards and requirements. The relevant evidentiary materials submitted, although touching upon the issue, do not clarify it. Before shipment, Qingdao Hisense International Marketing Co., Ltd. applied to the United States-based BEC Laboratory Electronics Company for certification of the products intended for export. BEC Laboratory Electronics Company conducted tests in accordance with relevant standards in the areas of radio spectrum, electromagnetic compatibility, safety, and human health effects. The certified wireless equipment and communication equipment complied with relevant directive requirements. Qingdao Hisense International Marketing Co., Ltd. also issued a declaration of conformity. The plaintiff had received the certification opinion and the declaration of conformity and did not raise objections to the testing content and standards. It can be inferred that if the defendant delivered to the plaintiff products of the same quality as the tested samples, the plaintiff would accept the quality of those products. However, the plaintiff claims that the products actually delivered by the defendant had issues of missing raw materials and components, constituting a product quality problem. The plaintiff's acceptance of the certification opinion submitted by the defendant does not automatically mean that the specifications/standards upon which the certification opinion was based constitute the quality requirements necessary to achieve the purpose of the contract. The technical standards and quality requirements for cross-border telecommunications products are relatively complex issues. This case lacks available technical specifications for resolution; it can only be decided and handled by applying rules of evidence and contract law based on the evidence submitted by the plaintiff and the defendant.

The plaintiff, Jiepao Company, primarily based its quality claims on the fact that the imported products were subject to a government-mandated recall in the importing country by the competent government authority. To protect the interests of unspecified consumers and public safety, under the guidance and supervision of government authorities and in accordance with prescribed procedures and requirements, manufacturers and importers shall promptly take recall measures for products with systemic defects, thereby effectively preventing and eliminating the harm that such defects may cause. This is an important measure implemented in many countries and regions, including China. Because product recalls are implemented under the guidance and supervision of government authorities, the product recall system is not entirely composed of civil actions between equal parties; its legal liabilities include not only civil liability but also administrative liability. Therefore, it is inappropriate to treat uniformly, according to the validity and evidentiary standards of civil acts, the process and legal liability of the recall of the plaintiff's imported products of the defendant, Hisense Company, mandated by government authorities in the plaintiff's country. This is also evidenced by the large amount of evidence submitted by the plaintiff in relation to the product recall, which includes participation of government authorities in risk assessment and notification of recall procedures. The defendant's challenge, solely on the grounds that the testing was commissioned unilaterally or not by an independent third party, is not objective. The main factual basis for the defendant's defense regarding product quality is the pre-shipment certification opinion. Although this certification opinion was issued by a certification body in a third country, it was essentially applied for by the defendant itself and does not inherently carry stronger probative value than the product testing accepted by the plaintiff in its own country. With respect to the two inspection reports generated before and after delivery of the products, the quality issues found in the testing of the imported products in Turkey were concentrated in areas such as electromagnetic compatibility, radio spectrum, and instruction defects. The technical standards and quality requirements relied upon in the relevant test reports are, in many respects, the same or substantially similar in expression to those relied upon in the product certification applied for by the defendant before shipment. It is precisely because of the comparability of the items and technical specifications between the two inspections that the changes in inspection results before and after delivery can more specifically reflect the quality condition of the products. However, the product recall system prevents harm from occurring or spreading through the recall itself, without requiring actual harm as a prerequisite. Moreover, the content of recall measures is diverse, including modification or supplementation of consumer instructions, repair, replacement, retrieval, and other forms. In this case, the inspection conducted during the recall process did not reach the conclusion that the plaintiff persistently argued during the litigation, namely, that there were manufacturing defects due to missing raw materials and components during the production process (in theory, product defects can be classified into design defects, manufacturing defects, instruction defects, and development defects). Furthermore, the degree of danger determined by the risk assessment of the defective products and the appropriateness of the recall measures commensurate with that degree of danger are not reflected in the recall evidence submitted by the plaintiff. In summary, the plaintiff's imported products were subject to a government-mandated recall in the importing country due to product defects. Although the defendant denied this, it has not produced evidence to rebut the fact asserted by the plaintiff. This case does not review the recall measures themselves. However, since the plaintiff primarily relies on the product recall as the basis for asserting contractual liability against the defendant, it is necessary to examine the relevance of the recall-related facts to the case and their relationship with the various conditions required for the establishment of the plaintiff's liability claims.

The recall system involves multiple legal relationships under the participation of regulators, implementers, consumers, facilitators, and others. Regarding the allocation and recovery of legal liability between the manufacturer and the importer as implementers, aside from relevant economic regulatory laws, it is essentially governed by the Contract Law, which regulates the legal relationships in international contracts for the sale of goods. When an importer bears product recall liability and subsequently seeks civil liability from the manufacturer, the contract of sale shall serve as the primary basis. For products such as mobile phones, which are relatively precise and require telecommunications technology, especially in cross-border telecommunications product sales, even if the product itself is free from defects or flaws, there is still some uncertainty as to whether it can be used normally and effectively. If the buyer and seller do not enter into a written contract to agree on technical standards and quality requirements for the products, product inspection, handling of quality issues, allocation of risks, and assumption of liabilities, then neither the plaintiff Jiepao Company's claim of ordering products based on discovering an exhibit at a trade fair, nor the defendant Hisense Company's claim that the parties entered into a supply contract with broad and simple terms, can substitute for a complete written sales contract with clear terms. This created a hidden risk of quality disputes arising from defects discovered after the products were placed into circulation in Turkey, and also created difficulties in resolving the dispute. Both the plaintiff and the defendant bear responsibility for this. With respect to risk prevention and control corresponding to product recalls, once the products have been exported, the party that is more convenient and effective in reasonably preventing and eliminating risks should take timely and appropriate measures. In this case, the products were shipped by air from Hong Kong on July 27, 2008, and the plaintiff cleared customs and imported them in Turkey on July 29, 2008. The opinion formed by the plaintiff after commissioning a chamber of commerce inspection was issued on February 3, 2009. The plaintiff then decided to recall the products voluntarily, and the government-mandated recall procedure was initiated in June 2009. Before the products were formally placed into circulation, necessary inspection should have been conducted to avoid the greater burden of a recall from consumers, and the manufacturer should have intervened in a timely manner to take necessary measures to prevent or eliminate harm that defects might cause, or the breaching party should bear civil liability for repair, replacement, or retrieval to mitigate losses for the manufacturer and importer. However, based on the evidence currently available in this case, the plaintiff did not exercise the necessary and prudent attention, leading to the serious consequence of consumer complaints in the circulation field and a government-mandated recall under regulatory supervision. This prevented the defendant, as the manufacturer, from participating in the recall procedures such as risk assessment, and objectively resulted in the most severe form of recall, i.e., retrieval, without any opportunity for response or remediation. As co-implementers of the recall measures, within their internal relationship, the plaintiff could have played a greater role in risk prevention and control but, for whatever reason, did not fully do so, thus bearing some responsibility for the final outcome. Even within the recall process, questions concerning the allocation of responsibility between the plaintiff and the defendant — such as the specific type of product defect (e.g., if it was a design defect and the manufacturer submitted a certification opinion to the importer without the importer objecting, then losses arising from the design defect cannot be entirely borne by the manufacturer), the degree of danger of the product, whether product retrieval was absolutely necessary, and the proportion of defective products to total products — were not sufficiently proven by the plaintiff's evidence. Therefore, the plaintiff shall bear corresponding civil liability for the outcome of the performance of this international sales contract.

Even if the defendant, Hisense Company, could rely on the pre-shipment certification opinion as factual basis for product conformity, the defendant also lacks sufficient grounds to rebut the fact that its exported products were subject to a mandatory recall in Turkey due to failing certain inspection items. When the probative value of evidence is roughly equal, taking delivery of the goods as the node, inspection conclusions after delivery are generally more capable of reflecting changes and developments than those before delivery, which also conforms to normal logical patterns. Therefore, for the actual situation of the product recall, it is appropriate for the defendant to bear more than half of the responsibility, consistent with the facts reflected by the evidence. Product recall focuses on prevention and elimination of danger, not on actual harm as a prerequisite. The plaintiff, Jiepao Company, has not sufficiently proven the actual harm caused by the product defects, and the plaintiff also bears some responsibility for the final outcome. Therefore, it is consistent with the actual circumstances of this case for the plaintiff to bear less than half of the responsibility. Given that there is only the result of the product recall without a more sufficient and reliable final conclusion fully attributing liability to the defendant, only the result of product retrieval without sufficient basis for the necessity of adopting this form, only an assessment opinion that the product has defects without a clear conclusion on the degree of harm of systemic defects and the scope of their existence, and also considering whether the defendant was given opportunities to participate, express views, and take corresponding measures during the recall process, as well as the measures the plaintiff should have taken for risk prevention and control, it is appropriate for the plaintiff and the defendant to bear 40% and 60% of the liability, respectively, for the claim for return of payment. If the plaintiff claims return of payment, naturally this corresponds to the return of the goods. This issue is not within the scope of the claims in this main action and counterclaim. It is also difficult to achieve simultaneous performance in a strict sense in actual enforcement. If a dispute arises over this issue during actual enforcement, it may be resolved separately based on the actual circumstances. In addition, the plaintiff also seeks to hold the defendant contractually liable on the ground that the defendant unilaterally entrusted the manufacturing of the products to a third party to complete. Since the plaintiff and the defendant did not explicitly agree to exclude actual production by a third party, the above ground raised by the plaintiff cannot be sustained. The plaintiff's request to use RMB as the currency for payment is granted, and conversion may be made according to the central parity rate of the US dollar against the RMB announced by the China Foreign Exchange Trade System authorized by the People's Bank of China on the date of payment (or corresponding extension) (on February 13, 2008, 1 USD = RMB 7.1952; on July 9, 2008, 1 USD = RMB 6.8632). The plaintiff's claim for interest to run from the actual date of payment does not conform to the legal characteristics of a sales contract. Interest on the loss shall be calculated from the date the plaintiff filed its claims with this court, with reference to the benchmark interest rate for loans of financial institutions for the corresponding period published by the People's Bank of China. The expenses for translation and notarization incurred by the plaintiff in this litigation are a direct result of the plaintiff's performance of litigation obligations. The plaintiff's claim that the defendant should bear these expenses is not supported by the Qingdao Intermediate People's Court of Shandong Province. The establishment of the counterclaim raised by the defendant requires that its products meet the requirements as a prerequisite. Its calculation of lost foreseeable profits also lacks factual basis. Regardless of whether the products were specifically researched, developed, and produced for the plaintiff, absent a clear special agreement, there is no basis within the legal relationship of a sales contract to require the plaintiff, as buyer, to bear research and development costs. The court does not support the defendant's counterclaim.

Accordingly, in accordance with Articles 111, 119(1), 148, and 155 of the Contract Law of the PRC, and Article 235 of the Civil Procedure Law of the PRC, the Qingdao Intermediate People's Court of Shandong Province rendered the following judgment on August 24, 2012:

I. The defendant (counterclaim plaintiff), Hisense Company, shall return to the plaintiff (counterclaim defendant), Jiepao Company, the payment for goods in the amount of RMB 614,030.4;

II. The defendant (counterclaim plaintiff), Hisense Company, shall compensate the plaintiff (counterclaim defendant), Jiepao Company, for the interest loss on the amount listed in item I above, calculated from August 17, 2009, with reference to the benchmark interest rate for loans of financial institutions for the corresponding period published by the People's Bank of China, up to the date this judgment takes effect;

III. The other claims of the plaintiff (counterclaim defendant), Jiepao Company, are dismissed;

IV. The counterclaim of the defendant (counterclaim plaintiff), Hisense Company, is dismissed.

The above sums shall be paid within ten days from the date this judgment takes effect. If the obligation to pay money is not fulfilled within the period specified in this judgment, interest on the debt for the period of delayed performance shall be doubled in accordance with Article 229 of the Civil Procedure Law of the PRC.

The court acceptance fee for the main action is RMB 14,716, of which the plaintiff (counterclaim defendant), Jiepao Company, shall bear RMB 5,886, and the defendant (counterclaim plaintiff), Hisense Company, shall bear RMB 8,830. The court acceptance fee for the counterclaim is RMB 4,457, to be borne by the defendant (counterclaim plaintiff).

After the first-instance judgment was pronounced, neither party filed an appeal, and the first-instance judgment has become legally effective.
